of personal or family history.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A college student and a man got into a fight at a bar while playing pool. The college student allegedly started the fight when he struck the man in the head with a pool cue after the man sank a game-winning shot. At the college student's trial for assault, the prosecution called one of the college student's classmates to testify. The classmate was not at the bar during the fight. The classmate offered to testify that the next day at class, he approached the college student and said, \"It was wrong of you to start a fight with the man over a game of pool,\" at which point the college student bowed his head and walked away. The classmate's testimony is A) admissible as a statement of the declarant's then-existing state of mind. B) inadmissible as character evidence. C) inadmissible as hearsay not within any recognized exception. D) admissible as a prior consistent statement. E) inadmissible as a statement that is not in the defendant's favor. F) inadmissible because the classmate was not an eyewitness to the fight. G) inadmissible as self-serving. H) admissible as part of the res gestae. I) admissible as an admission. J) admissible as a statement of personal or family history. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "A. admissible as a statement of the declarant's then-existing state of mind.", "answer_ms": 507}
{"id": "q0264", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 150.71, "n_chunks": 151, "scores": [0.3828, 0.3117, 0.3424, 0.3656, 0.4176, 0.3898, 0.3527, 0.4163, 0.4212, 0.3278, 0.3012, 0.4283, 0.297, 0.2541, 0.3427, 0.5733, 0.3061, 0.2849, 0.4615, 0.4224, 0.3754, 0.3224, 0.3871, 0.4345, 0.3846, 0.2189, 0.4036, 0.7133, 0.3355, 0.3502, 0.356, 0.3498, 0.3568, 0.4153, 0.2498, 0.3094, 0.3919, 0.4609, 0.5183, 0.4166, 0.3768, 0.3745, 0.4083, 0.2825, 0.3992, 0.401, 0.5408, 0.2868, 0.4422, 0.2501, 0.3036, 0.4552, 0.4381, 0.3608, 0.2903, 0.2861, 0.3831, 0.3079, 0.4552, 0.5184, 0.685, 0.4116, 0.4209, 0.3215, 0.3989, 0.461, 0.5211, 0.2373, 0.494, 0.4416, 0.3413, 0.3059, 0.4434, 0.3505, 0.3349, 0.3723, 0.4159, 0.3165, 0.3776, 0.4494, 0.4899, 0.3853, 0.3763, 0.3973, 0.3991, 0.3505, 0.6283, 0.4666, 0.394, 0.6242, 0.3677, 0.7803, 0.4785, 0.4127, 0.42, 0.3579, 0.3023, 0.3376, 0.6646, 0.5478, 0.4112, 0.474, 0.5631, 0.4874, 0.3284, 0.3597, 0.4028, 0.2948, 0.3163, 0.2898, 0.3574, 0.377, 0.3487, 0.4012, 0.3937, 0.4554, 0.4576, 0.5793, 0.4759, 0.3685, 0.3436, 0.4358, 0.36, 0.3594, 0.4485, 0.4922, 0.4679, 0.449, 0.4041, 0.4008, 0.2951, 0.3769, 0.3863, 0.4069, 0.3983, 0.3313, 0.3535, 0.5094, 0.4425, 0.4931, 0.4508, 0.5176, 0.3989, 0.5216, 0.3574, 0.4646, 0.4979, 0.4225, 0.2707, 0.6227, 0.4445], "eot_score": 0.7794, "eot_read_ms": 31, "reference_answer": "B. not recover, because the professor remains liable.", "query": "A landlord owned a three-story home in fee simple and leased it to his friend for a term of 10 years. By the terms of the lease, the friend expressly covenanted to pay a monthly rental of $300 and to pay the taxes on the premises during the term of the lease. The lease also stipulated that the friend, as lessee, may not assign or sublease the said premises. The friend and his family lived in the house for two years. Then the friend, still owing the landlord three months back rent, assigned his leasehold interest in the property to a professor, who was unaware of the prohibition against assignments. This written assignment expressly provided that the professor would pay the landlord the monthly rental of $300, but was silent concerning the payment of taxes. The landlord never objected to this assignment. Six months after the assignment, the landlord conveyed his property interest in the premises to a new owner. After residing in the house for a year, the professor subleased the third floor to a student for a term of two years; the agreement provided that the professor would receive a monthly rental payment of $100. After the student had made regular rental payments to the professor for the first six months of the sublease, he defaulted. The following month, the professor sent the new owner $200 as payment for rent and notified him that he should collect the remaining $100 from the student, who refused to vacate the premises. In an action by the new owner against the student to recover the additional $100 for rent due, he will most probably\n\nA. recover, even though he never objected to the sublease.\nB. not recover, because the professor remains liable.\nC. recover, because the new owner inherited all rights and responsibilities from the original landlord.\nD. recover, because the landlord and sublessee are in privity of estate.\nE. not recover, because the student is not directly responsible to the new owner.\nF. not recover, because the student was not in privity of contract with the new owner.\nG. not recover, because the professor would only be held liable.\nH. not recover, because the professor is the one who subleased to the student.\nI. recover, because the student is in direct violation of his sublease agreement.\nJ. recover, because the student defaulted on his rental payment.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A landlord owned a three-story home in fee simple and leased it to his friend for a term of ten years. By the terms of the lease, the friend expressly covenanted to pay a monthly rental of three hundred dollars and to pay the taxes on the premises during the term of the lease. The lease also stipulated that the friend, as lessee, may not assign or sublease the said premises. The friend and his family lived in the house for two years. Then the friend, still owing the landlord three months back rent, assigned his leasehold interest in the property to a professor, who was unaware of the prohibition against assignments. This written assignment expressly provided that the professor would pay the landlord the monthly rental of three hundred dollars, but was silent concerning the payment of taxes. The landlord never objected to this assignment. Six months after the assignment, the landlord conveyed his property interest in the premises to a new owner. After residing in the house for a year, the professor subleased the third floor to a student for a term of two years. The agreement provided that the professor would receive a monthly rental payment of one hundred dollars. After the student had made regular rental payments to the professor for the first six months of the sublease, he defaulted. The following month, the professor sent the new owner two hundred dollars as payment for rent and notified him that he should collect the remaining one hundred dollars from the student, who refused to vacate the premises. In an action by the new owner against the student to recover the additional one hundred dollars for rent due, he will most probably A) recover, even though he never objected to the sublease. B) not recover, because the professor remains liable. C) recover, because the new owner inherited all rights and responsibilities from the original landlord. D) recover, because the landlord and sublessee are in privity of estate. E) not recover, because the student is not directly responsible to the new owner. F) not recover, because the student was not in privity of contract with the new owner. G) not recover, because the professor would only be held liable. H) not recover, because the professor is the one who subleased to the student. I) recover, because the student is in direct violation of his sublease agreement. J) recover, because the student defaulted on his rental payment.", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The new owner won't be able to recover the $100 from the student. Since the student is just a sublessee, there's no direct legal connection between them and the new owner—so the professor still has to cover the full rent owed.", "expert_answer": "E) not recover, because the student is not directly responsible to the new owner.\n\nThe student is a sublessee, so there is no privity of estate or privity of contract between the student and the new owner. The professor remains liable to the new owner for the full rent.", "expert_latency_s": 5.93, "stall_ms": 29, "relay_ms": 1482}
